    "result_text": "The full test suite is green. There's only one test class in the project (`AcceptanceTest`), and it passes along with a successful build.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `customerCode` assignment for `POST /api/owners`, formatted `<LAST3>-<NNNN>`.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`Owner` entity** (`model/Owner.java`) \u2014 added a `customerCode` field mapped to `@Column(name = \"customer_code\")` with getter/setter.\n\n2. **New function** (`rest/function/owner/AssignCustomerCode.java`) \u2014 mutates the built `Owner` in place (via `@Val`), computing:\n   - `LAST3` = first three letters of `lastName`, upper-cased (safely handles shorter names).\n   - `NNNN` = `ownerRepository.findAll().size() + 1`, zero-padded to 4 digits \u2014 \"one more than the current number of owners\".\n\n3. **POST pipeline** (`officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml`) \u2014 inserted an `assignCustomerCode` step between `build` and `save`, under `govern: [ transaction ]` so the count and the persisted code share the write transaction. Running before `save` means the count reflects existing owners, giving each successive create a consecutive sequence number.\n\n4. **OpenAPI schema** (`openapi.yml`) \u2014 added a read-only `customerCode` property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries the field and MapStruct maps it by name into the GET response.\n\n5. **DB schemas** \u2014 added the `customer_code` column to the `owners` table in all four schema files (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres); hsqldb is what the tests use, the rest kept in sync.\n\nThe acceptance test verifies both the `SMI-`/`JON-` prefix format and that two consecutive creates produce consecutive sequence numbers \u2014 both pass.",
